You didn't want to talk. You unwillingly nodded in response with a dissatisfied grimace on your face.
"Had a rough day start?" the bearded man kept asking.
"Hell yeah," you snapped.
"Splendid!" cried the man, "you are a typical werewolf."
"What?" you didn't understand. 
"You see, I didn't work as a truck driver my whole life. Actually, I'm the doctor of philosophy. But living on a teacher's salary...," he sighed, "so seeing you, I somehow remembered a mythical creature. A werewolf."



"Nobody bit me," you said for unknown reason.
"That is not necessary to become one!" said the man being happy to find an interlocutor.
That was interesting and you became all ears.
"It is sufficient to put a wolf pelt on, pronounce a spell and the job is done."
A waitress brought a tea, but you thought about something more important. You recollected, that a few days ago visited a country house of an acquainted  of yours. And you actually tried a wolf pelt on there...
"And the most interesting," the philosopher continued, "the best way to kill such a werewolf is to drive a hawthorn stake into him. I see you don't want your tee. I'll drink it while it's warm." After those words the man took the glass and made a large gulp before you could stop him.
"Ah-h, wonderful. You should have drunk the tea, it's very good." You turned purple with rage, stood up and almost ran out of the bar, trying not to hear what your new acquainted was talking behind your back. You decided to take rest in the neighboring grove and in the evening go visit the scoundrel who had put a spell on you.  You hid in the shrubs and fell asleep, lulled by a peaceful buzzing of bees. When the kingdom of Morpheus released you, it was already dark. You was starving. You left your shelter and saw how the philosopher went out of the bar. The anger seize you like a whirlpool.